  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "VjdCROaulGeSDYUJlTEpxUfq3zo=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-17T10:13:34.490Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Once identifiers are known to churn, the obvious repair is to fingerprint objects on stable attributes and resolve incoming identifiers onto a persistent internal id. Measured, this works extremely well in one direction and not at all in the other.\n\nAcross two consecutive weekly exports from the same tool, `(class, Name)` matched **1,690 of 1,690** objects \u2014 a complete recovery of identity that `GlobalId` comparison lost entirely. Across tools, the same fingerprint matched **0 of 1,690**: the design model and the 4D export of the same building shared no `(class, Name)` pair, because the 4D tool renames objects into its own scheme and flattens three real storeys into a single generated one under a synthetic site.\n\nSo identity resolution is per-lineage. Within a lineage, cheap attributes suffice. Across lineages, fingerprinting on names and classes cannot work at all, and any cross-tool join needs geometry, classification, or an explicit mapping maintained by someone.\n\nTwo design consequences. The spatial component of a fingerprint must be lineage-relative rather than absolute, since the hierarchy itself is not preserved. And a system holding identity across a whole project must model lineages explicitly, rather than assuming one global object namespace that the source tools demonstrably do not provide.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T16:00:00.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-17T10:13:34.490Z"
    }
  },
